<h1>Modular Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A modular belt conveyor uses interlocking plastic belt modules that snap together with hinge rods to form the conveying surface. Each module is a molded plastic segment, typically polypropylene, polyethylene, or acetal, that links to adjacent modules to create a continuous belt of any required width and length. Modular belts dominate <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food processing</a>, beverage, and packaging applications where washdown capability, chemical resistance, and field-repairable belt construction outweigh the higher initial cost compared to <a href="/conveyor-types/flat-belt-conveyors/">flat belt conveyors</a>.</p>
<h2 id="how-heading">How Modular Belt Conveyors Work</h2>
<p>Modular belt conveyors drive the belt using sprockets rather than friction-drive pulleys. The sprocket teeth engage with the underside of the belt modules, positively driving the belt through the conveyor path. This positive drive eliminates belt slippage, the belt moves at exactly the speed the sprocket dictates. Sprocket drive also means the belt does not need high tension to maintain traction, reducing wear on the belt and frame components compared to friction-driven flat belts.</p>
<p>The belt rides on a wear strip support system, UHMW (ultra-high molecular weight polyethylene) strips or rails that run the length of the conveyor frame. The belt modules slide along these wear strips with low friction. Wear strips are replaceable and inexpensive, protecting the more costly frame structure from belt abrasion. Some heavy-duty modular belt conveyors use roller supports instead of wear strips to reduce friction for heavy product loads.</p>
<h3>Module Construction</h3>
<p>Each belt module is an injection-molded plastic piece with interlocking tabs on the leading and trailing edges. A hinge rod, typically a stainless steel or plastic pin, threads through aligned holes in the tabs of adjacent modules to connect them. The hinge rods allow each module row to articulate relative to the next, enabling the belt to flex around sprockets, navigate curves, and conform to conveyor frame geometry. Modules are assembled in a staggered (bricked) pattern, so narrow and wide modules combine to build almost any belt width.</p>
<h3>Belt Assembly and Repair</h3>
<p>Assembling a modular belt requires no special tools or adhesives. Modules interlock by hand or with simple fixtures, and hinge rods slide through to complete the connection. This assembly method is the primary advantage over fabric belts: a damaged section of modular belt is repaired by removing the hinge rods on either side of the damaged area, swapping out the bad modules, and reinserting the rods. The repair takes minutes and happens on the conveyor frame without removing the entire belt. Fabric belt repairs require vulcanizing, lacing, or complete belt replacement.</p>
<h2 id="surface-heading">Modular Belt Surface Types</h2>
<h3>Flush Grid</h3>
<p>Flush grid belts have a flat, smooth top surface with small openings at the hinge points between modules. The surface is nearly continuous, providing stable support for flat-bottomed products. Flush grid is the most common modular belt surface for general conveying of boxes, cartons, bottles, and packages. The small grid openings allow air circulation and water drainage, making flush grid belts suitable for washdown environments. Flush grid modules are available from every major modular belt supplier in a range of pitches, materials, and load ratings.</p>
<h3>Raised Rib</h3>
<p>Raised rib belts have longitudinal or diagonal ribs molded into the top surface. The ribs increase friction between the belt and products, preventing slipping on incline and decline conveyor sections. Ribs add some grip, but for real inclines the surface choice matters more. Forbo's <a href="https://www.forbo.com/media/document/8d828b24-c03a-498c-ab3b-713371a7a5d5" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Siegling Prolink design guide</a> gives rough achievable inclines of 3 to 5 degrees for a flat top surface and 20 to 40 degrees for a friction top surface, and recommends testing with the actual product. Raised rib belts suit applications where products need lateral stability, the ribs resist sideways sliding during curves and transfers.</p>
<h3>Perforated (Open Area)</h3>
<p>Perforated and open-grid modular belts have large open areas for drainage, air flow, or vacuum. Open area varies by series: Intralox lists 50 percent for its <a href="https://www.intralox.com/belt-finder/modular-plastic-belting/series-2200/radius-flush-grid" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Series 2200 Radius Flush Grid</a>. Food processing uses perforated belts under spray washers, blanchers, and coating applicators where liquid must drain through the belt. Cooling and drying applications use perforated belts to allow air to pass through the product layer. Vacuum transfer applications use perforated belts over vacuum chambers to hold lightweight products (labels, lids, pouches) against the belt surface.</p>
<h3>Non-Skid (Friction Top)</h3>
<p>Non-skid belts have rubber or silicone inserts molded into or bonded onto the module top surface. These inserts provide high friction grip for incline and decline applications where standard plastic surfaces lack sufficient traction. Forbo's rough guideline for friction top surfaces is 20 to 40 degrees. Steeper inclines need flights: Forbo gives under 60 degrees for straight profiles and under 90 degrees for bent profiles. Non-skid modules also suit wet or oily product environments where plastic-on-product friction drops. The rubber inserts are replaceable on some module designs, extending belt life in high-wear applications.</p>
<h3>Roller Top</h3>
<p>Roller top modular belts embed small rollers into the module surface, oriented perpendicular to belt travel. Products on roller top belts can slide laterally (across the belt) while the belt carries them forward. This enables zero-pressure accumulation, when products back up against a stop, they stop moving laterally while the belt continues underneath them. Roller top belts serve accumulation, diverting, and merging applications in packaging and distribution. <a href="/conveyor-types/activated-roller-belt/">Activated roller belt (ARB)</a> designs use powered rollers embedded in modular belt modules for active product diverting and sorting without traditional diverter hardware.</p>
<h2 id="curve-heading">Navigating Curves with Modular Belts</h2>
<p>Radius-rated modular belts handle curves. Straight-running modular series do not side-flex, so the belt series must be chosen for the layout. The individual modules articulate at each hinge point, allowing the belt to follow <a href="/conveyor-types/curved-conveyors/">curved conveyor</a> frames without the edge-stretching and tracking problems that plague fabric belts on curves. On a curve, the inside edge of the belt travels a shorter distance than the outside edge. Modular belt modules accommodate this differential through the hinge geometry, modules on the inside compress slightly while modules on the outside separate slightly at the hinge points.</p>
<p>Minimum curve radius depends on the belt series and is stated as a multiple of belt width, measured from the inside edge. Intralox lists 2.2 times belt width for its Series 2200 Radius Flush Grid. Its <a href="https://www.intralox.com/belt-finder/modular-plastic-belting/series-2400/tight-turning-radius" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Series 2400 Tight Turning Radius</a> belt runs 1.7 times belt width up to 18 in. wide, 1.8 times from 18 to 24 in., 2.0 times from 24 to 30 in., and 2.2 times from 30 to 36 in. On that belt, a 24-inch-wide belt needs an inside radius of about 43 inches. Tighter curves increase belt wear and require more frequent replacement of wear strips on the inside curve edge where friction concentrates.</p>
<p>Curve conveyors with modular belts eliminate the need for separate <a href="/conveyor-types/tabletop-conveyors/">tabletop chain conveyors</a> or dedicated curve sections. A single modular belt conveyor handles straight runs, curves, and elevation changes in one continuous belt path. This reduces the number of conveyor-to-conveyor transfers and the associated product handling risks (jams, misalignment, gaps) that occur at each transfer point.</p>
<p>Side-flexing modular belt chains (wide mat-top chains) combine the side-flexing capability of tabletop chains with the wider surface area and snap-together construction of modular belts. These hybrid designs suit applications that require tight curves (90-degree and 180-degree turns) at high speeds with positive product control.</p>
<h2 id="wash-heading">Washdown &amp; Sanitation</h2>
<p>Modular belts excel in washdown environments. The plastic modules resist water, cleaning chemicals, and sanitizers that degrade fabric belts. Each plastic has a different chemical resistance and temperature window, so check the belt maker's chemical resistance chart against your sanitation chemistry. The open construction of modular belts, gaps at hinge points and between modules, allows cleaning solutions to penetrate and flush contaminants from the belt surface and underside.</p>
<p><strong>Clean-in-place (CIP) capability</strong> is a major advantage. Modular belts can be cleaned on the conveyor frame without removal. High-pressure spray, foam cleaning, and chemical sanitizing reach all belt surfaces because the belt is not a continuous sealed membrane. Fabric belts trap contaminants between the top cover and carcass layers, a problem modular belts avoid because each module is a solid, non-layered piece of plastic.</p>
<p><strong>FDA and USDA compliance</strong> drives modular belt selection in <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food processing</a>. Major modular belt manufacturers offer belt materials that meet FDA food-contact regulations such as <a href="https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/cdrh/cfdocs/cfcfr/CFRSearch.cfm?fr=177.1520" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">21 CFR 177.1520</a> (olefin polymers, which covers polypropylene and polyethylene) and USDA acceptance for meat and poultry processing. Color-coded modules (typically blue or white for food applications) enable visual contamination detection, a foreign plastic fragment from a blue belt is visible on food products, unlike fragments from a clear or food-colored belt.</p>
<p><strong>Metal-detectable modules</strong> contain additives that make the plastic detectable by standard metal detection equipment used in food processing lines. If a module fragment breaks off into the product stream, downstream metal detectors catch the contamination. Metal-detectable modules are offered by major modular belt suppliers for food safety-critical applications.</p>
<p><strong>Conveyor frame sanitary design</strong> complements the belt sanitation features. Stainless steel frames with open construction, rounded edges (no square corners that trap debris), sloped surfaces for drainage, and tool-free belt removal mechanisms complete the washdown conveyor system. Sideguards on modular belt conveyors must also meet sanitary requirements, food-grade plastic guards with smooth surfaces and quick-release mounting are standard on food-grade modular belt conveyors.</p>
<h2 id="mfg-heading">What Actually Matters When You Specify Modular Belt</h2>
<p><strong>Module geometry:</strong> Flush-grid, raised-rib, friction-top, roller-top, and closed-top designs behave very differently. Start with how the product sits on the belt, whether drainage is needed, and whether the belt must accumulate, divert, or index product without slip.</p>
<p><strong>Material and temperature:</strong> Polypropylene, polyethylene, and acetal each have different chemical resistance, impact performance, and temperature limits. For its Series 2200 Radius Flush Grid, Intralox lists continuous ranges of 34 to 200°F for polypropylene, -50 to 150°F for polyethylene, and -50 to 200°F for acetal. The right plastic depends on washdown chemistry, freezer or oven exposure, and whether the line runs dry or wet.</p>
<p><strong>Sprocket and tracking design:</strong> Modular belts solve many tracking issues that plague flat belts, but only when sprocket engagement, shaft spacing, returnway support, and thermal expansion are handled correctly. Forbo's design guide gives a formula for belt length and width change with temperature, which matters on hot-fill, oven, and freezer lines. The support structure matters as much as the belt itself.</p>
<p><strong>Repairability and spare strategy:</strong> One of the biggest advantages of modular belt is field repair. Specify how quickly individual modules, rods, sprockets, and wear strips can be replaced, and keep spare modules and sprockets on hand for the line's critical widths.</p>
