<h2 id="apps-heading">Modular Conveyor Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Meat and poultry processing:</strong> Modular belts are the standard conveying surface in meat and poultry plants. Flush grid and perforated belts carry raw product through cutting, trimming, marinating, and packaging operations. The belts endure daily high-pressure washdown with hot water and chemical sanitizers. USDA-accepted materials and metal-detectable options meet regulatory requirements for direct food contact.</p>
<p><strong>Beverage packaging lines:</strong> Modular belt conveyors transport bottles, cans, and cartons through filling, capping, labeling, and packing operations. The positive sprocket drive maintains precise belt speed for synchronized operations. Curve-capable modular belts thread through beverage line layouts that pack multiple 90- and 180-degree turns into tight floor space. <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/beverage/">Tabletop chain conveyors</a> compete in this application for single-file container handling.</p>
<p><strong>Baking and snack food:</strong> Perforated modular belts carry bakery products through proofers, coolers, and freezers. The open belt surface allows air to circulate around products for uniform heating and cooling. Plastic modules have temperature limits well below metal belts, so hot zones such as ovens usually use <a href="/conveyor-types/wire-mesh-conveyors/">wire mesh</a> while modular belts serve proofers, coolers, and freezers within the plastic's rated range. Cooling spirals in bakeries often use plastic modular belts because radius-rated modules handle the helical path.</p>
<p><strong>Pharmaceutical and cosmetics:</strong> Modular belts in pharmaceutical packaging convey bottles, vials, tubes, and cartons through filling, capping, labeling, and cartoning equipment. The chemical resistance of plastic modules withstands cleaning agents used in pharmaceutical manufacturing. White or light-colored modules enable visual inspection of the belt surface for contamination.</p>
<p><strong>Parcel and e-commerce sorting:</strong> Roller-top modular belts enable zero-pressure accumulation and active diverting in parcel sorting systems. Activated roller belt (ARB) technology uses modular belt modules with embedded powered rollers to divert packages to specific lanes without traditional diverter hardware. These systems handle the high throughput and gentle handling requirements of e-commerce fulfillment operations.</p>
